@page "/per-component"

<PageTitle>Blazor Render Modes Comparison</PageTitle>

<h1>Blazor Render Modes Comparison</h1>

<p>This page demonstrates the different Blazor render modes and how long each takes to become interactive. Notice the timing differences:</p>

<div class="row">
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <RenderModeTimer Label="Static Rendering (Server-side only)">
            <CounterStatic />
        </RenderModeTimer>
    </div>
    
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <RenderModeTimer Label="Interactive Server">
            <CounterInteractiveServer />
        </RenderModeTimer>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="row">
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <RenderModeTimer Label="Interactive WebAssembly (with prerender)">
            <CounterWebAssembly />
        </RenderModeTimer>
    </div>
    
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <RenderModeTimer Label="Interactive WebAssembly (no prerender)">
            <CounterWebAssemblyNoPrerender />
        </RenderModeTimer>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="mt-4">
    <h3>Understanding the Results:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Static Rendering:</strong> Fastest to display, but no interactivity (buttons won't work)</li>
        <li><strong>Interactive Server:</strong> Quick to become interactive, requires server connection</li>
        <li><strong>WebAssembly (prerendered):</strong> Shows content quickly, but takes time to become interactive</li>
        <li><strong>WebAssembly (no prerender):</strong> Slower to show content, but becomes interactive immediately when displayed</li>
    </ul>
    
    <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">
        <strong>Note:</strong> The WebAssembly components need to download and initialize the .NET runtime in the browser, 
        which is why they take longer to become interactive. The prerendered version shows static content first, 
        while the non-prerendered version waits until everything is ready.
    </div>
</div>
